Did you mean to put this bit on `Element` and not `Document`? Having it here on Element means you can open a new popover from within the events for another popover, e.g.,

```
<div id="popoverA" popover="auto">A</div>
<div id="popoverB" popover="auto">B</div>
<script>
popoverA.addEventListener('beforetoggle', (e) => {
  popoverB.showPopover(); // This runs while popover A is half way open
});
popoverA.showPopover();
<script>
```

Here, popoverB tries to close popoverA before popoverA has finished opening. I think the end state is maybe ok in this case, but it makes me nervous that this is a path to bugs. It seems like maybe this bit should live on `Document`, next to "hiding popover nesting count"?

Or I'm entirely missing the nuance of the new algorithms.

I'm pretty sure that test is wrong. Walking through it:

```html
<div id=p34 popover=auto>p34
  <div id=p35 popover=auto>p35</div>
  <div id=p36 popover=hint>p36</div>
</div>
```

```js
p34.showPopover();
p35.showPopover();
p36.showPopover();

assert_true(p34.matches(':popover-open'), 'p34 should be open');
assert_true(p35.matches(':popover-open'), 'p35 should be open');
assert_true(p36.matches(':popover-open'), 'p36 should be open');
```

All good, we're in this state:

```mermaid
graph TD
    p34 --> p35
    p34 --> p36
```

Then:

```js
await clickOn(p36);

assert_true(p34.matches(':popover-open'), 'p34 should remain open');
assert_false(p35.matches(':popover-open'), 'p35 should be light dismissed');
assert_true(p36.matches(':popover-open'), 'p36 should remain open, because its ancestor p34 is still open');
```

All good. Clicking the hint should close non-parent autos, and that's what happened. We're in this state:

```mermaid
graph TD
    p34 --> p36
```

But then:

```js
p34.hidePopover();
assert_false(p34.matches(':popover-open'), 'p34 should be closed');
assert_true(p36.matches(':popover-open'), 'p36 should be left open');
```

"p36 should be left open" seems incorrect. Its parent (p34) has been closed. It should close too.

Chrome's behaviour of nulling out the hint stack parent during the click creates weird bugs. Sorry - another video with sound:

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a8362ef0-3c9e-4508-a1ce-35e167be9312

I wonder if that test was supposed to be doing something else. From the test name, it isn't clear to me what it's meant to be testing.
